What an Analyst Should Look for in DNS, HTTP, HTTPS/TLS, ARP, ICMP and SMB Traffic
1. Overview table
Protocol
Normal behaviour
Suspicious behaviour
DNS (UDP/TCP 53)
Short queries for known domains. Fast replies from the company resolver. Most queries are A, AAAA, MX and PTR.
Very long or random-looking subdomain names (data hidden in DNS). Many NXDOMAIN replies (malware generating domains). Large TXT replies. Queries sent straight to unknown external DNS servers. Same domain queried at fixed intervals.
HTTP (TCP 80)
GET and POST requests to known sites with a normal browser User-Agent. Reasonable response codes.
Passwords or tokens in clear text. Requests to a bare IP address. Fixed-interval requests (beaconing). Unusual User-Agent such as a scripting tool. Downloads of .exe, .ps1 or .zip from unknown hosts. Many 401/403/404 codes (guessing or scanning).
HTTPS / TLS (TCP 443)
Valid, trusted certificate. Server name (SNI) matches a known site. Modern TLS version.
Self-signed, expired or mismatched certificates. TLS on unusual ports. Connections to a raw IP with no SNI. Very regular small sessions to one host. Old protocol versions or odd cipher choices.
ARP
One request and one reply when a host needs a neighbour's MAC. Few packets per host.
One IP address answered by two different MAC addresses (spoofing). Floods of unrequested (gratuitous) ARP replies. A host asking for every address on the subnet (scan).
ICMP
Occasional small echo requests and replies. Destination unreachable messages after real errors.
Echo requests to many addresses in order (ping sweep). Unusually large payloads or data hidden in echo packets (tunnelling). Many port-unreachable messages (UDP scan).
SMB (TCP 445)
File and printer sharing between internal clients and file servers. Authenticated sessions.
SMB leaving the network to the internet. One host connecting to many hosts (lateral movement). Many failed logons. Access to ADMIN$ or IPC$ shares. Old SMBv1 in use. Large transfers at odd hours.


2. What to inspect for each protocol
DNS: query name length, record type, response code, how often one host asks. Filter: dns, dns.flags.rcode != 0.
HTTP: method, URL, Host header, User-Agent, response code, file type. Filter: http.request, http.user_agent contains "curl".
TLS: certificate details and the Client Hello server name. Filter: tls.handshake.type == 1.
ARP: pairs of IP and MAC addresses. Wireshark flags duplicates under Analyze > Expert Information. Filter: arp.duplicate-address-detected.
ICMP: type and code (type 8 echo request, type 0 reply, type 3 unreachable), packet size and the number of destinations. Filter: icmp.
SMB: commands, share names and authentication results. Filter: smb || smb2.
